# config/spawn-memory-cap - the memory ceiling every crewmate, scout, and
# secondmate this home launches runs under (bin/fm-spawn.sh wraps each launch in
# bin/fm-memcap.sh, which puts the agent in its own systemd user scope). Copy
# this into a local, gitignored config/spawn-memory-cap and replace the value
# below with your own.
#
# A single value, read from the first non-empty, non-comment line; blank lines
# and full-line "#" comments are ignored. It is either:
#
#   <n>[K|M|G|T]   an absolute ceiling, systemd's 1024-based suffixes (e.g. 6G)
#   <n>%           a percentage of this host's RAM, 1-100 (e.g. 40%)
#   off            no ceiling at all - the pre-existing behavior
#
# An ABSENT file does NOT mean "no cap": it means the built-in default, which is
# 40% of RAM for a ship task and 25% for a scout or a secondmate. That split is
# deliberate - a ship task builds and runs test suites inside its ceiling, while
# a scout reads code and a secondmate supervises. A value that cannot be parsed
# warns and falls back to that same default rather than silently removing the
# protection the typo was asking for.
#
# One value covers every kind here on purpose; per-kind tuning lives in the
# built-in defaults, which already scale with the size of the machine.
# FM_SPAWN_MEMORY_CAP overrides this file for a single spawn.
#
# A host that cannot create a systemd user scope (no systemd, no user manager,
# or a delegated cgroup without the memory controller) still spawns, unwrapped,
# and says so in the pane. Containment is never allowed to become a spawn
# failure, so setting a value here is a request, not a guarantee.
#
# The value below is an illustrative placeholder - pick a ceiling that leaves
# room for the heaviest legitimate thing your agents run (a full test suite, a
# production build) while staying well under what would starve this machine.
40%
